    "available_at": "2026-11-26T19:25:41.344916+00:00",
    "body": "Planet One Group chairman Sanjeev Mansotra (left) shakes hands with Senegalese President Bassirou Diomaye Faye. Planet One and state miner Miferso plan to mine iron ore at Falémé and produce steel in Senegal as part of a $933-million project. Credit: Planet One Group\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 26, 2026\nPlanet One Group and Senegalese state miner Miferso laid the foundation stone Thursday for a $933-million (C$1.33-billion) project to mine iron ore at Falémé and make steel for the country’s builders and manufacturers.\nThe deposits lie in the Kédougou region, about 750 km southeast of Dakar. The first stage would cost about $200 million and produce 3 million–5 million tonnes of iron ore a year, Planet One Chairman Sanjeev Mansotra told Reuters. He put peak output at 10 million tonnes a year.\n“The Ministry will ensure strict compliance with the commitments made regarding local content, employment opportunities for young people in the region, transparency, and environmental protection, particularly the preservation of the Falémé River,” Mines and Geology Minister Cheikhou Oumar Seck said in Planet One’s release.\nSenegal imported 722,000 tonnes of iron and steel in 2025, up 75% from a year earlier, according to the national statistics agency. The partners plan to use natural gas to turn the ore into direct-reduced iron, a feedstock for steelmaking. The government is still working to secure affordable gas for the plants, Planet One said.\nHigh grade\nBased in the United Arab Emirates, Planet One holds 80% of the venture. Miferso and the Senegalese government each hold 10%, Mansotra told Reuters. He expects debt to cover 60% of the investment and equity to cover the rest. The first stage, including mine equipment and upgrades to roads and rail, would take 12 to 18 months. Planet One expects the full project to create more than 1,200 direct and indirect jobs.\nPlanet One estimates the three deposits hold up to 600 million tonnes of iron ore. It gave no estimate date, resource category or overall grade. Earlier work put the iron grade at 59% in hematite at Koudékourou and an average of 43% in magnetite at Goto and Farangalia, according to a December 2025 report from Senegal’s Extractive Industries Transparency Initiative.\nInfrastructure\nMiferso plans a new rail line of about 310 km to connect Falémé with the national network at Tambacounda so ore can reach the coast. The state miner still listed the rail link as planned in its Nov. 9 update.\nArcelorMittal (NYSE: MT) had aimed to ship Falémé ore to its European mills. Its $2.2-billion project, agreed with Senegal in 2007, called for a new port near Dakar and first production in 2011. An arbitration tribunal ruled in 2013 that Senegal could terminate the agreements. ArcelorMittal agreed in May 2014 to pay Senegal $150 million to settle the dispute.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Iron Ore\nREGION: Africa\nCOMPANY: Miferso Planet One Group\nRepublish this article",
